From d6fbd1044a036b3bdd010ca22274a0aaba1e4b14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 8 Jun 2026 16:51:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 03/83] libc: MB_CUR_MAX must reflect the locale (1), not a
 hardcoded 4
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

<stdlib.h> hardcoded `#define MB_CUR_MAX 4`, but substrate's libc is a
single-byte "C" locale: __ctype_get_mb_cur_max() returns 1 and mbtowc/mblen
are single-byte.  The constant 4 advertised multibyte support the libc does
not provide, so wide-character-aware clients keyed on MB_CUR_MAX took their
wchar_t path against a single-byte locale.

The visible casualty was CDE's dtterm: with MB_CUR_MAX==4 it stored each
terminal cell as a wchar_t and rendered the line via XwcDrawString, so every
ASCII cell drew as one real glyph followed by three NUL "tofu" boxes
("r###o###o###t###@###a###" for "root@agar").  Its menu bar was fine because
Motif draws single-byte via XmbDrawString.

Define MB_CUR_MAX as the standard accessor (__ctype_get_mb_cur_max()) so it
tracks the real locale — now 1, so dtterm takes the single-byte XmbDrawString
path and renders cleanly, and it would adapt automatically if substrate ever
grows real multibyte locale support.  No substrate source uses MB_CUR_MAX in a
constant-array context, so the function form introduces no VLA breakage.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 include/stdlib.h | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/include/stdlib.h b/include/stdlib.h
index 16dbe5ff..7f7a2de5 100644
--- a/include/stdlib.h
+++ b/include/stdlib.h
@@ -11,7 +11,17 @@ extern "C" {
 /* wchar_t comes from stddef.h above. */
 
 #define RAND_MAX 2147483647
-#define MB_CUR_MAX 4
+
+/* MB_CUR_MAX must reflect the current locale's maximum multibyte length.
+ * substrate's libc is a single-byte "C" locale (mbtowc/mblen are single-byte;
+ * __ctype_get_mb_cur_max() returns 1).  Hardcoding 4 lied about multibyte
+ * support the libc does not actually provide: wide-character-aware clients
+ * keyed on MB_CUR_MAX (notably CDE's dtterm, which then stores cells as
+ * wchar_t and draws via XwcDrawString) rendered every ASCII cell as one glyph
+ * followed by three NUL "tofu" boxes.  Use the accessor so MB_CUR_MAX tracks
+ * the real locale (and would adapt automatically if multibyte support lands). */
+extern int __ctype_get_mb_cur_max(void);
+#define MB_CUR_MAX (__ctype_get_mb_cur_max())
 
 #define EXIT_SUCCESS 0
 #define EXIT_FAILURE 1
